Consider the exclusive electron-induced single-nucleon knockout reaction A(e,e'p)B, in which an incoming electron of four-momentum k scatters off a nucleus of mass number A, transferring four-momentum q = k - k' to the system and ejecting a proton of four-momentum p_N, leaving a residual nucleus B in a discrete state. In the conventional analysis the differential cross section is written in factorized form as a product of an off-shell electron-nucleon cross section sigma_{eN} (depending on a choice of single-nucleon electromagnetic current, e.g. CC1, CC2, CC3) and a reduced cross section / spectral function S(p_m, E_m); a nuclear transparency T is then defined as the ratio of measured to plane-wave (or distorted-wave) calculated yields. Both S and T depend strongly on the choices made for the current operator, the optical potential describing final-state interactions (FSI), and the prescription used to define T.

Formulate and develop a fully unfactorized framework for computing the exclusive cross section d^6 sigma / (d epsilon' d Omega_{e'} d Omega_N dE_N) directly from a many-body matrix element of the nuclear electromagnetic current, satisfying the following requirements:

(i) Relativity is incorporated consistently, with bound-state wave functions, ejectile distorted waves, and the electromagnetic current treated within a Dirac (or equivalent fully relativistic) formalism;

(ii) Final-state interactions between the outgoing nucleon and the residual nucleus are included via a (complex, possibly nonlocal) optical potential, without resorting to eikonal or factorization approximations;

(iii) Spin-orbit components of both the bound-state mean field and the final-state optical potential are retained, and their effect on the cross section and on polarization/asymmetry observables is quantified;

(iv) The framework produces direct exclusive cross sections that can be compared to data across a range of kinematics (parallel, perpendicular, quasielastic and beyond) without introducing model-dependent quantities such as a reduced cross section or a transparency.

Prove or demonstrate that the resulting unfactorized predictions are stable under changes of the single-nucleon current prescription, and quantify the residual model dependence due to the choice of optical potential and bound-state wave function.

